I wrote:

>: I think alignments should stay as they are now. They add spice to the
>game [...]

Someone else wrote:

>I disagree.  Chaotics are grossly crippled, and I rarely act
>chaotic (slaying peacefuls etc.) anyway, so why should I have
>to stick with an alignment that gives *ME* no advantages and a
>whole passel of disadvantages.  Sure, I can convert, but it is
>a real pain in the neck.

If you have a close look at the yet finished parts of the WCST spoilers
8.00, you'll se that the (dis)advantages of being either lawful or
chaotic are exactly balanced. For each thing that would harm a lawful
alignment, there is something that benefits a chaotic one, and vice
versa. So being lawful or chaotic has exactly the same number of
(dis)advantages.

So, lawfuls are just as "crippled" as chaotics. I agree with you,
though, that neutrals are in an advantageous position. Thus, I repeat
my suggestion about the Helmet of Opposite Alignment: Either remove it
from the game completely, or make it of less use, by having it damage
your alignment when you have already identified it. Maybe you should
have a standing with EACH of the three gods, that would be lowered when
you would knowingly wear or remove a HoOA. No god would like it when
you knowingly deserted him, would they?

So my suggestion: when you put on or off a HoOA, the god you formerly
followed should frown on you, since you knowingly and willingly desert
him or her. This is already the case when you sacrifice a unicorn to
change alignment, so it would be logical and in the spirit of the game.
Of course, if you have not already identified the AoOA, there is no
reason to punish the player for wearing it, since he did not convert
knowingly.

But keep the rest of the alignment system the same, please, with no
choice for the player in this.

